Id: acc028w
Severity: WARN, INFO
Category: accounts
Check: check_accounts
Fix: Ask the account's owner why; remove the link or object so the shell writes a plain history file again, or accept it where no history is the policy.
References: bash(1), HISTFILE; zsh(1), HISTFILE; ksh(1), HISTFILE; tcsh(1), savehist
Controls: CIS 3.3, 5.1; NIST AC-2, AC-3; ISO 5.16, 5.18, 8.3; CE User access control

A shell history file in the listed login ID's home (.bash_history,
.sh_history, .zsh_history, .history, .ash_history, .histfile) is not a
regular file. The shell writes what the account typed to it at the end
of each session, which is often the only record of what an account did
on a host without process accounting or auditd; an intruder who has
taken an account replaces the file with a symbolic link to /dev/null,
a directory or a FIFO so that nothing is kept, and sometimes with a
link to a file of theirs so that the history goes where the account's
owner will not look.

A link to /dev/null is reported as INFO: it is the usual way for a user
or an administrator to keep no history on purpose, and worth knowing
about but no fault in itself. Anything else, a link to another place,
a directory, a FIFO or a device, is WARN until the account's owner
explains it. A history that is a regular file, present or absent, is
not reported. Root and the accounts from UID_MIN (login.defs; else
1000, or 500 on macOS) up are looked at, when they have a login shell
and a home that exists.
